---
/**
 * ExplainBack — the optional one-sentence "why does this work?" note
 * (`docs/m8-gamification.md`, M8.3; spec §7's `explainPrompt` frontmatter field,
 * §6's `progress:v1:{slug}` record).
 *
 * Props:
 * - `slug`   the lesson that owns the record the note is stored in.
 * - `prompt` the lesson's own `explainPrompt`. Rendered VERBATIM as the field's
 *            `<label>`: it is the question, and a component that decorated it
 *            would be answering for the author.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS. Self-explanation (Chi et al.) and elaborative interrogation: the
 * reader states, in their own words, why the thing they just read works — and
 * meets that sentence again at review time, where it is both a retrieval cue and
 * a calibration check ("still agree?"). It is the only mechanic in this product
 * that stores free-form reader text.
 *
 * IT EARNS NOTHING, and that is load-bearing rather than modest: no stage, no
 * pip, no tally, no count, no schedule move (`writeNote` in
 * `src/lib/progress.ts` stamps nothing). Skipping it costs the reader exactly
 * nothing, which is the only honest way to make a prompt optional; grading a
 * sentence — even by counting one — would turn elaboration into a chore and put
 * a second currency beside the mastery states the design allows.
 *
 * THE FOUR OBLIGATIONS this component carries (the design's word for why it is
 * not one control but four): a **Delete** button beside Save, because a privacy
 * promise with no deletion path is an erosion of it; the note NAMED in the reset
 * control's copy (`/learn` composes that sentence from `countNotes`); the
 * device-scope label adjacent to the textarea, not buried in a footer; and the
 * `note` field carried through every other write of the record, which
 * `parseMastery`'s unknown-field bag already does for free.
 *
 * WHY THE MARKUP LIVES IN A `<template>`. The box appears only once the reader
 * has said they finished the lesson — self-explanation of a lesson nobody has
 * read yet is a prompt with no referent — and "appears" has to mean it is not
 * there beforehand: a hidden textarea is still a form field in the DOM, and a
 * hidden prompt is still a question the page is asking. A `<template>` is inert
 * in every mode that matters (not rendered, not focusable, not in the
 * accessibility tree, and its content is not in the document tree at all), so the
 * copy, the ids and the styling stay reviewable in this file while the page a
 * reader meets before completing carries no note box whatsoever.
 *
 * THIS IS THE ONE CONTROL IN THE PRODUCT THAT APPEARS WITH PROGRESS. Everything
 * else M8 added is a display: `docs/m8-gamification.md` is emphatic that stage is
 * never a permission, and `tests/e2e/m8-calm-invariants.spec.ts` pins that as
 * "a Mastered lesson offers exactly what an untouched one offers" on the `arrays`
 * lesson — which is why `arrays` deliberately authors no `explainPrompt`.
 * Authoring one there is a design decision that reopens that invariant (the note
 * is not an unlock — nothing is gated behind it and it grants nothing — but it IS
 * a control that was not there before), not a bug fix to make quietly.
 *
 * A11Y:
 * - A real `<label>` bound to the textarea by `for`/`id`, carrying the authored
 *   question as its whole text, so the field's accessible name IS the prompt.
 * - `maxlength` on the field (the store bounds it again — see `NOTE_MAX_CHARS`),
 *   with a visible character counter that is `aria-hidden`: a counter that
 *   announced on every keystroke would talk over the reader as they typed.
 *   The count reaches the live region only at the two moments it becomes news —
 *   the last stretch, and the cap — and each is announced once.
 * - ONE polite `role="status"`, shipped EMPTY, carrying both that counter news
 *   and the save/delete confirmation. The confirmation is text PLUS a glyph, and
 *   never colour: the glyph is `aria-hidden` decoration beside a sentence that
 *   says the same thing.
 * - Delete is `aria-disabled` rather than `disabled` while there is nothing to
 *   delete (M7.1 CMP-3): it is exactly where focus stands the instant a note is
 *   removed, and a real `disabled` would drop that focus onto `<body>`.
 *
 * JS-OFF / STORAGE BLOCKED: with no script the template is never cloned, so
 * there is no field, no button and no prompt anywhere on the page — and the
 * `<noscript>` kill-switch below covers the class besides, the belt-and-braces
 * rule `ReviewStrip` follows for the same reason. With storage blocked, `readNote`
 * and `isComplete` both answer "nothing", so the box never appears rather than
 * appearing and promising a save this device cannot keep.
 */
import { NOTE_MAX_CHARS } from '../lib/progress';
import { nextUid } from './uid';

interface Props {
  slug: string;
  prompt: string;
}

const { slug, prompt } = Astro.props;

// Fail the BUILD on an authoring slip rather than rendering an empty label: a
// blank prompt is a labelled field with no question in it, which no reader can
// answer and no test can distinguish from a missing component.
if (prompt.trim().length === 0) {
  throw new Error(
    `ExplainBack: explainPrompt is empty for "${slug}" — remove the field or author a question (spec §7).`,
  );
}

const uid = nextUid();
const fieldId = `explain-${uid}`;
const countId = `${fieldId}-count`;
const scopeId = `${fieldId}-scope`;
---

<template data-explain-template data-slug={slug}>
  <div class="explain-back" data-explain-back>
    {
      /* The review replay, populated and revealed by the island only on a
        `?review=1` visit that has something to replay. It leads the block on
        purpose: meeting your own earlier sentence BEFORE you edit it is what
        makes the box a calibration check rather than a blank page. */
    }
    <p class="explain-back__recall" data-explain-recall hidden></p>

    <label class="explain-back__label" for={fieldId}>{prompt}</label>

    <textarea
      class="explain-back__field"
      id={fieldId}
      data-explain-field
      rows="3"
      maxlength={NOTE_MAX_CHARS}
      aria-describedby={`${countId} ${scopeId}`}></textarea>

    <p class="explain-back__meta">
      {
        /* Both halves of this line are the field's `aria-describedby`, so a
          screen-reader user meets the remaining count and the device scope when
          they focus the box. The count is `aria-hidden` so it is not ALSO read
          as loose text while browsing, and a description is spoken on focus
          rather than on change — which is what keeps the counter from
          chattering; the live region below carries it only when it becomes
          news. */
      }
      <span
        class="explain-back__count"
        id={countId}
        data-explain-count
        aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="explain-back__scope" id={scopeId}>
        Saved only in this browser. Never sent anywhere.
      </span>
    </p>

    <div class="explain-back__actions">
      <button type="button" class="btn-secondary" data-explain-save>
        Save note
      </button>
      {
        /* BESIDE Save, never buried: the ethics review's requirement, and the
          reason it is `aria-disabled` rather than `disabled` is that this is
          where focus stands the moment a note goes. */
      }
      <button
        type="button"
        class="btn-secondary"
        data-explain-delete
        aria-disabled="true"
      >
        Delete note
      </button>
    </div>

    {
      /* Ships EMPTY so nothing is announced on load, and stays in the DOM: a
        live region has to be in the accessibility tree before its first
        population for that announcement to fire reliably. */
    }
    <p class="explain-back__status" role="status" data-explain-status>
      {
        /* The glyph is wrapped rather than carrying `hidden` itself: `hidden` is
          an HTML attribute, not an SVG one, so the wrapper is what the script
          toggles — and Tailwind's preflight hides `[hidden]` with `display:
          none !important`, which beats the inline-flex below. */
      }
      <span class="explain-back__status-icon" data-explain-status-icon hidden>
        <svg
          width="16"
          height="16"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
          <path d="M4 12l5 5L20 6"></path>
        </svg>
      </span>
      <span data-explain-status-text></span>
    </p>
  </div>
</template>

<noscript>
  <style>
    /* Kill-switch, as every M8 component ships one. Belt-and-braces here, like
       `ReviewStrip`'s: the box only ever exists because the island cloned the
       template, so a JS-off page cannot show one. The rule is what makes that
       structural fact unbreakable if the block is ever server-rendered — and
       `!important` because a kill-switch must be unbeatable by any
       equal-specificity rule in the bundle (QA DEFECT-1). */
    .explain-back {
      display: none !important;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<style is:global>
  /* GLOBAL, for the reason `ReviewStrip`'s block records: Astro scopes a
     component's CSS by stamping `data-astro-cid-*` onto the elements it renders,
     and everything here is cloned out of a `<template>` by script. Every
     selector is therefore namespaced under `.explain-back`, and nothing in this
     block declares `background-color`, `border-color` or `box-shadow` — it is
     unlayered, so it would beat `@layer components`' state rules from the
     resting state (the trap `LessonCard` documents). */
  .explain-back {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  /* The reader's own earlier words, quoted back. A left rule and the muted role
     make it read as a quotation rather than as authored copy — the same idiom
     the lesson body gives a blockquote. */
  .explain-back__recall {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  /* The authored question, at the weight of a control's label rather than a
     heading: this block titles itself (it has no heading of its own, so it can
     never leave one stranded — the rule `nojs-orphan-sections.spec.ts` guards). */
  .explain-back__label {
    display: block;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .explain-back__field {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    background-color: var(--surface-sunken);
    color: var(--text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    resize: vertical;
    transition: border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .explain-back__field:hover {
    border-color: var(--brand);
  }

  .explain-back__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .explain-back__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* The shared disabled recipe every aria-disabled control here uses (M7.1
     CMP-3), so "there is nothing to delete" looks the same as every other
     unavailable control on the site. */
  .explain-back [aria-disabled='true'] {
    opacity: var(--disabled-opacity);
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }

  /* Text AND a glyph, never colour: the confirmation reads the same in forced
     colours, in greyscale and to a screen reader (which gets the text alone —
     the glyph is aria-hidden). */
  .explain-back__status {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* The margin is unconditional, unlike MarkComplete's `:empty` trick, because
     this region is never empty in the DOM sense — it holds the glyph wrapper and
     the text span from the start, and it must: a live region that is only
     inserted (or un-hidden) in the same frame as its first message is the classic
     way to lose an announcement. With no message the two spans generate no line
     box, so the block itself is zero-height and all this leaves under the buttons
     is --space-3 of breathing room. */
  .explain-back__status-icon {
    display: inline-flex;
    flex: none;
  }

  /* On paper a device-local control has no meaning, the same reason
     `.whats-next` and the practice footer are dropped (global.css print rules). */
  @media print {
    .explain-back {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * The Explain-it-back island: mount the box once the reader says they are
   * done, then read, save and delete their note.
   *
   * Every storage rule lives in `src/lib/progress.ts` — the key, the cap, the
   * read-modify-write that keeps the rest of the record intact, and the delete
   * that takes exactly one field — so this file only decides WHEN the box exists
   * and WHAT the reader is told.
   */
  import {
    deleteNote,
    isComplete,
    NOTE_MAX_CHARS,
    onRestore,
    readNote,
    writeNote,
  } from '../lib/progress';

  /** How few characters may be left before the count becomes worth announcing. */
  const ANNOUNCE_FROM = 20;

  const template = document.querySelector<HTMLTemplateElement>(
    '[data-explain-template]',
  );
  const slug = template?.dataset['slug'] ?? '';

  if (template && slug) {
    /**
     * Whether this visit arrived from the review strip.
     *
     * Read, never written: `?review=1` is a URL, not a preference (spec §6
     * permits no key for it), so a review visit leaves the device byte-identical.
     */
    const isReview =
      new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('review') === '1';

    /** The cloned box, once it exists. */
    let box: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let field: HTMLTextAreaElement | null = null;
    let recall: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let count: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let removeBtn: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let statusText: HTMLElement | null = null;
    let statusIcon: HTMLElement | null = null;
    /** The last character-count band announced, so it is said once per crossing. */
    let announcedBand = '';
    /**
     * Whether the box holds typing that has not been saved.
     *
     * It exists so a repaint can never overwrite the reader's unsaved sentence:
     * `paint()` runs on the completion click too (the same delegated listener
     * that reveals the box), and a reader who types first and un-ticks
     * completion second would otherwise watch their words be replaced by
     * whatever storage last held.
     */
    let dirty = false;

    /**
     * Says one thing, politely, in the single live region — with the glyph only
     * where the sentence is a confirmation.
     *
     * Rewriting a region with an identical string re-announces it, so an
     * unchanged message is left alone (the rule `PracticeCheck` follows).
     */
    function say(message: string, confirmed = false): void {
      if (!statusText || statusText.textContent === message) return;
      statusText.textContent = message;
      if (statusIcon) statusIcon.hidden = !confirmed || message === '';
    }

    /**
     * Paints the counter, and announces it only when it becomes news.
     *
     * Two moments qualify: entering the last {@link ANNOUNCE_FROM} characters,
     * and reaching the cap. Everything between is visible and silent — a live
     * region that spoke on every keystroke would talk over the reader mid-word.
     */
    function paintCount(): void {
      if (!field || !count) return;
      const left = NOTE_MAX_CHARS - field.value.length;
      count.textContent = `${left} characters left`;
      const band = left === 0 ? 'full' : left <= ANNOUNCE_FROM ? 'near' : '';
      if (band === announcedBand) return;
      announcedBand = band;
      if (band === 'full') say('No characters left in this note.');
      else if (band === 'near') say(`${left} characters left.`);
      else say('');
    }

    /** Reflects the stored note onto the field, the replay and Delete. */
    function paint(): void {
      if (!field) return;
      const stored = readNote(slug);
      // Unsaved typing wins over storage: see `dirty`. Everything else on the
      // block still repaints, so Delete and the replay stay true either way.
      if (!dirty) field.value = stored ?? '';
      removeBtn?.setAttribute('aria-disabled', stored ? 'false' : 'true');
      if (recall) {
        // The replay is the payoff, so it appears where the payoff is: on a
        // review visit, with something to replay. On an ordinary visit the
        // reader's words are already in the box below it, and quoting them
        // twice would be noise.
        const replay = isReview && stored;
        recall.textContent = replay ? `You wrote last time: “${stored}”` : '';
        recall.hidden = !replay;
      }
      paintCount();
    }

    /**
     * Clones the box into the page, once, and wires it up.
     *
     * Inserted after the template so it lands where this component was mounted —
     * below "What's next", clear of the button that reveals it, so nothing the
     * reader is looking at moves under them when it appears.
     */
    function mount(): void {
      if (box) return;
      const fragment = template!.content.cloneNode(true) as DocumentFragment;
      const root = fragment.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-explain-back]');
      if (!root) return;

      field = root.querySelector<HTMLTextAreaElement>('[data-explain-field]');
      recall = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-explain-recall]');
      count = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-explain-count]');
      removeBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-explain-delete]');
      statusText = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
        '[data-explain-status-text]',
      );
      statusIcon = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
        '[data-explain-status-icon]',
      );
      const saveBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-explain-save]');
      if (!field || !saveBtn || !removeBtn) return;

      field.addEventListener('input', () => {
        dirty = true;
        // A confirmation describes the note as it was SAVED, so editing makes
        // it stale: clear it rather than leave "Saved" beside text that is not.
        if (statusIcon && !statusIcon.hidden) say('');
        paintCount();
      });

      saveBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const typed = field!.value.trim();
        if (typed.length === 0) {
          // Nothing to keep, and nothing claimed: an empty box is not a delete
          // (Delete is its own button, beside this one) and not a save either.
          say('Nothing to save yet — the note is empty.');
          return;
        }
        // The store returns what actually landed, so a blocked or full device
        // gets the honest sentence rather than a promise (MarkComplete's rule).
        const saved = writeNote(slug, typed);
        announcedBand = '';
        if (saved === null) {
          // Still dirty: nothing landed, so the reader's text stays theirs to
          // copy elsewhere rather than being tidied away by the next repaint.
          say('Not saved — this browser is not letting the page store it.');
          return;
        }
        dirty = false;
        paint();
        say('Saved. Only in this browser.', true);
      });

      removeBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (removeBtn!.getAttribute('aria-disabled') === 'true') return;
        const gone = deleteNote(slug);
        announcedBand = '';
        // A delete the reader asked for empties the box, typing included: that
        // is the act, and leaving their words on screen beside "deleted" would
        // contradict it.
        dirty = false;
        paint();
        // Focus stays on this button (it is `aria-disabled`, never `disabled`),
        // so the reader who just deleted their note is not dropped onto <body>.
        say(
          gone
            ? 'Note deleted from this browser.'
            : 'Not deleted — this browser is not letting the page change it.',
          gone,
        );
      });

      template!.insertAdjacentElement('afterend', root);
      box = root;
      paint();
    }

    /** Takes the box away again — nothing stored, nothing to show. */
    function unmount(): void {
      box?.remove();
      box = null;
      field = null;
      recall = null;
      count = null;
      removeBtn = null;
      statusText = null;
      statusIcon = null;
      announcedBand = '';
      dirty = false;
    }

    /**
     * The reveal rule: the reader has said they finished this lesson, OR they
     * have a note here already.
     *
     * The second half is what keeps their words reachable after they un-tick
     * completion — changing your mind about finishing a lesson must not hide the
     * sentence you wrote, or the Delete button that removes it.
     *
     * Both halves are storage READS, so a blocked store answers "no" and the box
     * stays away rather than appearing and promising a save it cannot keep.
     */
    function sync(): void {
      if (isComplete(slug) || readNote(slug) !== null) {
        if (box) paint();
        else mount();
      } else {
        unmount();
      }
    }

    sync();

    // MarkComplete emits no event, so its act is observed the way the design
    // specifies: one delegated click listener. A listener on `document` runs in
    // the BUBBLE phase, after the button's own handler, so the completion this
    // click wrote is already in storage by the time this re-reads it.
    document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      const target = event.target instanceof Element ? event.target : null;
      if (target?.closest('[data-mark-complete]')) sync();
    });

    // A back/forward-cache restore replays no script, so a reader who cleared
    // their progress on `/learn` and came Back would otherwise meet a box the
    // device no longer has anything for.
    onRestore(sync);
  }
</script>
